WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.651
Promotion Discussion.

2
00:00:01.931 --> 00:00:05.835
Day 87 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.585 --> 00:00:08.609
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:12.196
Promotions are rarely given for doing your current job well.

5
00:00:12.476 --> 00:00:18.236
They come from showing you already work at the next level, and from making sure the people who decide can see it.

6
00:00:18.516 --> 00:00:24.383
Ask early, agree on criteria, gather evidence, and get your manager to argue your case for you.

7
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:26.501
Info:

8
00:00:26.781 --> 00:00:28.871
How this differs from related pages.

9
00:00:29.151 --> 00:00:41.098
Performance Review covers your rating, Appraisal Discussion covers the hike, this page covers moving up a level: the months-long conversation that leads to it, and what to do when the answer is not yet.

10
00:00:42.448 --> 00:00:43.301
Part 1.

11
00:00:43.581 --> 00:00:45.352
How promotions actually work.

12
00:00:46.602 --> 00:00:47.519
Step 1:

13
00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:51.405
Criteria: the company's level definitions (career ladder).

14
00:00:51.685 --> 00:00:52.602
Step 2:

15
00:00:52.882 --> 00:00:57.255
Evidence: sustained work at the next level, usually 6 to 12 months.

16
00:00:57.535 --> 00:00:58.453
Step 3:

17
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:03.021
Sponsor: your manager (and ideally a skip-level) who argues your case.

18
00:01:03.301 --> 00:01:04.261
Step 4:

19
00:01:04.541 --> 00:01:09.405
Committee or calibration: managers compare cases; often you're not in the room.

20
00:01:09.685 --> 00:01:10.645
Step 5:

21
00:01:10.925 --> 00:01:14.317
Budget and timing: cycles, headcount, open roles.

22
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.952
The key insight: you're not in the room when the decision is made.

23
00:01:19.232 --> 00:01:24.075
Your job is to give your manager a case so clear they can argue it in two minutes.

24
00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:26.299
Part 2.

25
00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.606
What typically changes between levels.

26
00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.221
Here is a quick table:

27
00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:34.573
From, then To, and The shift, and Evidence that shows it.

28
00:01:34.853 --> 00:01:36.133
Junior, then Mid.

29
00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:37.267
The shift:

30
00:01:37.547 --> 00:01:40.107
From needs guidance to works independently.

31
00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:41.773
Evidence that shows it:

32
00:01:42.053 --> 00:01:44.549
Delivers features end to end with little help.

33
00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:46.045
Mid, then Senior.

34
00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:47.179
The shift:

35
00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:49.741
From delivers tasks to owns outcomes.

36
00:01:50.021 --> 00:01:51.408
Evidence that shows it:

37
00:01:51.688 --> 00:01:55.720
Owns a service or feature area; makes good design calls; mentors.

38
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.771
Senior, then Staff or Lead.

39
00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:58.904
The shift:

40
00:01:59.184 --> 00:02:02.597
From owns a component to shapes the team or several teams.

41
00:02:02.877 --> 00:02:04.264
Evidence that shows it:

42
00:02:04.544 --> 00:02:09.984
Drives cross-team technical direction; multiplies others; solves ambiguous problems.

43
00:02:10.264 --> 00:02:11.907
Engineer, then Manager.

44
00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:13.040
The shift:

45
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.432
From my output to the team's output.

46
00:02:15.712 --> 00:02:17.099
Evidence that shows it:

47
00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:21.923
Has led people informally; handles conflict; hires; gives feedback well.

48
00:02:23.273 --> 00:02:23.913
Tip:

49
00:02:24.193 --> 00:02:25.579
Get your company's ladder.

50
00:02:25.859 --> 00:02:29.529
Ask your manager or HR for the written level expectations.

51
00:02:29.809 --> 00:02:33.969
If there are none, write down together what the next level means at your company.

52
00:02:34.249 --> 00:02:37.769
Without written criteria, the decision becomes subjective.

53
00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:40.015
Part 3.

54
00:02:40.295 --> 00:02:42.855
The promotion conversation, step by step.

55
00:02:44.105 --> 00:02:44.745
1.

56
00:02:45.025 --> 00:02:48.182
Starting the conversation (6 to 12 months before).

57
00:02:48.462 --> 00:02:49.401
You could say:

58
00:02:49.681 --> 00:02:51.793
I'd like to work towards Senior Engineer.

59
00:02:52.073 --> 00:02:56.617
Could you help me understand what the gap is between where I am now and that level?

60
00:02:56.897 --> 00:03:01.889
I'd like to agree on 2 to 3 concrete things I can work on, and check in on them regularly.

61
00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:06.503
Ask this early, not two weeks before the promotion cycle.

62
00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:08.557
2.

63
00:03:08.837 --> 00:03:10.287
Agreeing on criteria.

64
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:11.506
You could say:

65
00:03:11.786 --> 00:03:21.343
So, to summarise: lead the design and delivery of the delivery-slot service, mentor at least one engineer, and lead incident response independently.

66
00:03:21.623 --> 00:03:24.866
If I do those well by June, would that make a strong case?

67
00:03:25.146 --> 00:03:27.215
Can I write this down and share it with you?

68
00:03:28.565 --> 00:03:31.829
Then send a short email so you both have a written record.

69
00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:33.926
3.

70
00:03:34.206 --> 00:03:35.422
Regular check-ins.

71
00:03:35.702 --> 00:03:36.641
You could say:

72
00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:38.393
Quick promotion check-in:

73
00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:42.043
I've completed the slot-service design and it's in production.

74
00:03:42.323 --> 00:03:44.329
Mentoring with Kavya is going well.

75
00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:48.427
On a scale of 1 to 10, how close am I to the case we discussed?

76
00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:49.625
What's missing?

77
00:03:50.975 --> 00:03:53.471
Do this monthly or quarterly in your one-on-one.

78
00:03:54.821 --> 00:03:55.525
4.

79
00:03:55.805 --> 00:03:57.042
Ask for a sponsor.

80
00:03:57.322 --> 00:03:58.261
You could say:

81
00:03:58.541 --> 00:04:00.909
Would you be willing to put me forward in this cycle?

82
00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:03.962
I've drafted a promotion document with the evidence.

83
00:04:04.242 --> 00:04:06.973
Could you review it and tell me what would make it stronger?

84
00:04:08.323 --> 00:04:09.987
Also ask for visibility:

85
00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:13.893
Could I present the slot-service design at the architecture review?

86
00:04:14.173 --> 00:04:17.373
Committee members should know your work before your name comes up.

87
00:04:18.723 --> 00:04:19.491
5.

88
00:04:19.771 --> 00:04:21.713
Asking directly at cycle time.

89
00:04:21.993 --> 00:04:22.931
You could say:

90
00:04:23.211 --> 00:04:25.622
The promotion cycle opens next month.

91
00:04:25.902 --> 00:04:29.721
I believe I'm working at the senior level, and I'd like to be considered.

92
00:04:30.001 --> 00:04:34.033
Here's my summary of the evidence against the criteria we agreed on.

93
00:04:34.313 --> 00:04:37.001
Do you agree, and are you comfortable nominating me?

94
00:04:38.351 --> 00:04:39.268
Part 4.

95
00:04:39.548 --> 00:04:41.681
The promotion document (brag doc).

96
00:04:42.931 --> 00:04:47.070
Make your manager's job easy: one page, mapped to the criteria.

97
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.999
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

98
00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:53.948
It runs to 24 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

99
00:04:54.228 --> 00:04:57.599
Promotion case, Ashish Gupta, then Senior Engineer.

100
00:04:57.879 --> 00:04:59.351
Summary (3 lines).

101
00:04:59.631 --> 00:05:01.316
Evidence against criteria.

102
00:05:04.166 --> 00:05:05.126
Part 5.

103
00:05:05.406 --> 00:05:06.942
When the answer is not yet.

104
00:05:08.192 --> 00:05:09.301
Get specifics.

105
00:05:09.581 --> 00:05:10.520
You could say:

106
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.403
I'm disappointed, but I want to use this well.

107
00:05:13.683 --> 00:05:15.731
What specifically was missing in my case?

108
00:05:16.011 --> 00:05:19.467
Was it the evidence, the criteria, or timing and budget?

109
00:05:20.817 --> 00:05:22.651
Those are three different problems:

110
00:05:24.001 --> 00:05:25.367
Here is a quick table:

111
00:05:25.647 --> 00:05:27.887
Reason, and What it means, and Your move.

112
00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:29.169
Evidence gap.

113
00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:30.345
What it means:

114
00:05:30.625 --> 00:05:32.673
You aren't yet doing the next-level work.

115
00:05:32.953 --> 00:05:33.764
Your move:

116
00:05:34.044 --> 00:05:36.689
Agree on concrete projects that close the gap.

117
00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:38.057
Visibility gap.

118
00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:39.233
What it means:

119
00:05:39.513 --> 00:05:41.753
You did it, but the deciders didn't see it.

120
00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:42.844
Your move:

121
00:05:43.124 --> 00:05:46.239
Present work, write docs, get skip-level exposure.

122
00:05:46.519 --> 00:05:47.777
Budget or timing.

123
00:05:48.057 --> 00:05:48.953
What it means:

124
00:05:49.233 --> 00:05:51.089
Case is fine, there's no slot.

125
00:05:51.369 --> 00:05:52.180
Your move:

126
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.892
Get the commitment in writing for the next cycle.

127
00:05:56.242 --> 00:05:57.309
Get a commitment.

128
00:05:57.589 --> 00:05:58.527
You could say:

129
00:05:58.807 --> 00:06:02.711
If I close those gaps, can we agree I'll be put forward next cycle?

130
00:06:02.991 --> 00:06:06.917
I'd like to write down the specific goals and review them every month together.

131
00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:10.464
Repeated \not yet\.

132
00:06:10.744 --> 00:06:11.683
You could say:

133
00:06:11.963 --> 00:06:16.293
This is the second cycle where the goals were met but the promotion didn't happen.

134
00:06:16.573 --> 00:06:22.952
I want to grow here, and I'd like to understand honestly whether a promotion is realistic in the next 6 months.

135
00:06:23.232 --> 00:06:25.301
If not, I'd appreciate knowing why.

136
00:06:26.651 --> 00:06:28.187
Ask honestly and calmly.

137
00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:34.867
If the honest answer is no, you have a decision to make: see Resignation, and leave well if that's your choice.

138
00:06:36.217 --> 00:06:37.135
Watch out:

139
00:06:37.415 --> 00:06:38.076
Don't.

140
00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:42.473
Compare yourself to named colleagues (Rahul got promoted, why not me?).

141
00:06:42.753 --> 00:06:46.849
Use a resignation threat as a lever, unless you're truly ready to leave.

142
00:06:47.129 --> 00:06:50.479
Go silent and resentful; ask for specifics instead.

143
00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:54.407
Expect a promotion for tenure alone (I've been here 4 years).

144
00:06:55.757 --> 00:06:56.695
Part 6.

145
00:06:56.975 --> 00:06:58.213
After you're promoted.

146
00:06:59.463 --> 00:07:02.535
Thank your manager and sponsors, and the people who helped.

147
00:07:02.815 --> 00:07:03.476
Ask:

148
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.084
What does great look like at this new level, in the first 6 months?

149
00:07:07.364 --> 00:07:09.540
Expect to feel out of your depth at first.

150
00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.145
That's normal; it's what growth feels like.

151
00:07:12.425 --> 00:07:14.281
Help someone else get their promotion.

152
00:07:14.561 --> 00:07:17.015
Sponsoring others is part of being senior.

153
00:07:18.365 --> 00:07:19.303
Part 7.

154
00:07:19.583 --> 00:07:21.653
For leads: sponsoring your people.

155
00:07:22.903 --> 00:07:26.700
Have the career conversation with every report at least twice a year.

156
00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.308
Turn the level definitions into concrete, personal goals.

157
00:07:30.588 --> 00:07:36.817
Create opportunities: give stretch projects and visibility, and mention their names in rooms they're not in.

158
00:07:37.097 --> 00:07:39.337
Be honest about the gap and about timing.

159
00:07:39.617 --> 00:07:42.199
False hope damages trust more than not yet.

160
00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:47.023
Write the case together, and tell them the outcome personally before any announcement.

161
00:07:48.373 --> 00:07:49.247
Part 8.

162
00:07:49.527 --> 00:07:50.914
Practice conversation:

163
00:07:51.194 --> 00:07:52.623
Asking about promotion.

164
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:55.900
Here is how the conversation goes.

165
00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.569
I'd like to work towards Senior Engineer.

166
00:07:58.849 --> 00:08:00.769
Could you help me understand the gap?

167
00:08:01.049 --> 00:08:01.753
Sure.

168
00:08:02.033 --> 00:08:03.740
Your technical work is strong.

169
00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.177
The gap is ownership: driving a project end to end.

170
00:08:07.457 --> 00:08:11.297
Could I own the delivery-slot service, from design to launch?

171
00:08:11.577 --> 00:08:13.369
That would be a great opportunity.

172
00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:16.319
The conversation continues.

173
00:08:16.599 --> 00:08:22.317
If I deliver that well by June and mentor one new engineer, would that make a strong case?

174
00:08:22.597 --> 00:08:23.919
Yes, I think so.

175
00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:24.946
Great.

176
00:08:25.226 --> 00:08:28.554
I'll write this down and send it to you, so we're aligned.

177
00:08:28.834 --> 00:08:30.818
Can we check progress monthly?

178
00:08:31.098 --> 00:08:32.207
Let's do that.

179
00:08:33.557 --> 00:08:35.093
That's the end of this lesson.

180
00:08:35.373 --> 00:08:40.045
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

181
00:08:40.325 --> 00:08:46.427
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

